  "slug": "networks-omics-7167",
  "summary": "Reliable use of inferred dependency structure in omics integration requires comparison and significance testing at the level of whole topological structure.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of topology-aware statistics to compare and validate structure in high-dimensional data. A topologically invariant permutation test compares functional data using 2-Wasserstein distances on persistent diagrams with heat kernel smoothing. A separate preprint proposes Mahalanobis whitening before dimensionality reduction, motivated by Bures distance, to de-individualize fMRI signals. A published comparison sets topological data analysis against multivariate information theory for higher-order interactions, using toy shapes and human fMRI data. What is new is the emphasis on formal, whole-structure comparison rather than network construction alone. These items are heterogeneous and do not directly address omics network inference, so the shared thread is methodological: testing whether structural differences or interactions are real before interpreting them.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-13"
}
